**Mountebank vs WireMock**

| Feature | Mountebank | WireMock |
|---------|------------|----------|
| **Protocols** | HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, SMTP | HTTP, HTTPS |
| **Configuration** | JSON or JavaScript | Java API, JSON |
| **Stateful** | Yes (with inject) | Yes (scenarios) |
| **Performance** | Very good | Excellent |
| **Learning Curve** | Medium | Low |
| **Ecosystem** | Smaller | Large |
| **Record/Playback** | Yes | Yes |

**Key Insight:** Mountebank's true strength is its protocol diversity. When you need to mock non-HTTP services—legacy TCP systems, SMTP servers, or custom protocols—Mountebank is often the only open-source option. Its JavaScript injection system provides unlimited flexibility, though it requires more setup than HTTP-only tools.

Service virtualization goes beyond simple mocking to create complete, realistic simulations of entire systems. These patterns address common challenges in enterprise testing.

**Virtualization Maturity Model**

```
Level 1: Static Stubs
├── Hardcoded responses
├── One response per request
└── No state

Level 2: Parameterized Stubs
├── Response varies by input
├── Basic templating
└── Stateless

Level 3: Stateful Mocks
├── Maintains session state
├── Multi-step flows
└── Scenario-based

Level 4: Recorded Virtual Services
├── Captured from real traffic
├── Accurate timing simulation
└── Complex behavior patterns

Level 5: Intelligent Virtual Services
├── Machine learning for behavior
├── Anomaly simulation
└── Performance modeling
```

**Pattern 1: Contract-First Virtualization**

Create mocks from API specifications before the real API exists:

```yaml
# OpenAPI specification
openapi: 3.0.0
paths:
  /users:
    get:
      responses:
        200:
          description: List of users
          content:
            application/json:
              schema:
                type: array
                items:
                  $ref: '#/components/schemas/User'
    post:
      requestBody:
        required: true
        content:
          application/json:
            schema:
              $ref: '#/components/schemas/UserInput'
      responses:
        201:
          description: User created
```

```java
// Generate mock from OpenAPI
WireMock.fromOpenApi("user-api.yaml")
    .stubForAny()
    .withDefaultResponses()
    .withRandomData(true)
    .register();
```

**Pattern 2: Proxy-Based Recording**

Capture real traffic to create accurate mocks:

```java
// Record mode
WireMockServer wireMock = new WireMockServer(options()
    .port(8080)
    .recordMappings(true)
    .usingFilesUnderDirectory("recordings"));

wireMock.start();
wireMock.stubFor(any(anyUrl())
    .willReturn(aResponse()
        .proxiedFrom("https://production-api.example.com")));

// Run your tests against localhost:8080
// All traffic is recorded

// Stop recording
wireMock.stopRecording();

// Now you have recorded stubs that you can replay
```

**Pattern 3: Behavior Simulation**

Simulate realistic service behavior including failures and delays:

```java
// Realistic service behavior
stubFor(get("/api/service")
    .willReturn(aResponse()
        .withStatus(200)
        .withFixedDelay(100 + new Random().nextInt(50))  // 100-150ms
        .withHeader("X-Response-Time", "{{random value min=100 max=150}}")
        .withTransformers("response-template")));

// Failure injection
stubFor(get("/api/unstable")
    .inScenario("Chaos Testing")
    .willReturn(aResponse()
        .withStatus(200))
    .willSetStateTo("Healthy"));

stubFor(get("/api/unstable")
    .inScenario("Chaos Testing")
    .whenScenarioStateIs("Healthy")
    .willReturn(aResponse()
        .withStatus(503)
        .withBody("Service Unavailable"))
    .willSetStateTo("Unhealthy"));

stubFor(get("/api/unstable")
    .inScenario("Chaos Testing")
    .whenScenarioStateIs("Unhealthy")
    .willReturn(aResponse()
        .withStatus(200))
    .willSetStateTo("Recovered"));
```

**Pattern 4: Performance Profile Simulation**

Simulate different performance characteristics:

```java
// Simulate different performance profiles
public class PerformanceSimulator {

    private static final Map<String, Distribution> PROFILES = Map.of(
        "fast", new NormalDistribution(50, 10),
        "normal", new NormalDistribution(200, 50),
        "slow", new NormalDistribution(1000, 200),
        "timeout", new ConstantDistribution(5000)
    );

    public static void simulateProfile(String profile) {
        Distribution dist = PROFILES.get(profile);

        stubFor(any(anyUrl())
            .willReturn(aResponse()
                .withStatus(200)
                .withRandomDelay(dist)));
    }

    public static void simulateDegradation(int baseLoad) {
        // Gradually increase latency based on request count
        stubFor(any(anyUrl())
            .willReturn(aResponse()
                .withStatus(200)
                .withTransformers("response-template")
                .withTransformerParameter("baseLoad", baseLoad)
                .withBody("""
                    {
                        "latency": {{math (requestCount) "*" 1.5}},
                        "count": {{requestCount}}
                    }
                    """)));
    }
}
