**Pattern 10: Virtual Service Composition**

Compose complex scenarios from simple virtual services:

```java
public class VirtualServiceComposition {

    private final WireMockServer wireMock;
    private final List<VirtualService> services = new ArrayList<>();

    public VirtualServiceComposition(int port) {
        this.wireMock = new WireMockServer(options().port(port));
    }

    public void addService(VirtualService service) {
        services.add(service);
    }

    public void start() {
        wireMock.start();

        // Configure routing based on service path
        for (VirtualService service : services) {
            wireMock.stubFor(requestMatching(new ServiceMatcher(service.getPath()))
                .willReturn(aResponse()
                    .withTransformers("service-composition")
                    .withTransformerParameter("service", service)));
        }
    }

    private static class ServiceMatcher extends RequestMatcherExtension {
        private final String path;

        ServiceMatcher(String path) {
            this.path = path;
        }

        @Override
        public MatchResult match(Request request, Parameters parameters) {
            return MatchResult.of(request.getUrl().startsWith(path));
        }
    }
}

interface VirtualService {
    String getPath();
    Response execute(Request request);
}
```

**Key Insight:** Service virtualization patterns evolve from simple stubs to intelligent simulations. The right pattern depends on your testing goals: use contract-first for development, recording for accuracy, chaos patterns for resilience, and composition for complex scenarios. The investment in sophisticated virtualization pays off in faster, more reliable, and more comprehensive testing.

One of the most important decisions in API testing is when to use mocks and when to test against real services. The right balance ensures fast, reliable tests that still catch real integration issues.

**The Testing Spectrum**

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    MOCK TO REAL SPECTRUM                            │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                   │
│  Full Mocks                                  Full Real Services  │
│  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────►  │
│                                                                   │
│  [Unit]─────[Component]─────[Integration]─────[E2E]─────[Prod]  │
│                                                                   │
│  Speed: Fast                                   Speed: Slow       │
│  Isolation: High                               Isolation: Low    │
│  Cost: Low                                      Cost: High        │
│  Realism: Low                                   Realism: High     │
│                                                                   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**When to Mock**

```
✅ MOCK WHEN:
   - Service is unavailable or unstable
   - Service charges per call
   - You need to test error conditions
   - Service is not yet built
   - Tests must run offline
   - You need deterministic results
   - Testing in parallel (avoid rate limits)
   - Simulating rare edge cases

❌ AVOID MOCKS WHEN:
   - Testing actual integration behavior
   - Validating security/compliance
   - Performance testing with realistic load
   - Contract validation with provider
   - Service behavior is too complex to mock
```

**The Mocking Trap**

Mocks can create false confidence:

```java
// BAD: Mock that's too perfect
stubFor(get("/users/1")
    .willReturn(okJson("{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"John\"}")));

// Test passes, but real API might:
// - Return different field names
// - Have different authentication
// - Return errors for valid requests
// - Have different pagination
// - Behave differently under load
```

**The Hybrid Approach**

Test with both mocks and real services at different levels:

```java
public class HybridTestingStrategy {

    // Level 1: Unit tests with mocks (fast, many)
    @Test
    public void testUserServiceLogic() {
        // Mock the external dependency
        stubFor(get("/users/1").willReturn(okJson("{\"id\":1}")));

        User user = userService.getUser(1);
        assertEquals(1, user.getId());
    }

    // Level 2: Integration tests with real service (fewer)
    @Test
    @EnabledIfEnvironmentVariable(named = "RUN_INTEGRATION_TESTS", matches = "true")
    public void testRealUserService() {
        // Call actual staging API
        User user = realUserService.getUser(1);
        assertNotNull(user);
        assertNotNull(user.getName());
    }

    // Level 3: Contract tests (verify mock matches real)
    @Test
    public void testMockMatchesReal() {
        // Get response from real API (once)
        Response realResponse = callRealAPI();

        // Configure mock to match
        stubFor(get("/users/1")
            .willReturn(aResponse()
                .withStatus(realResponse.getStatus())
                .withBody(realResponse.getBody())));

        // Verify mock responses match real
        Response mockResponse = callMockAPI();
        assertEquals(realResponse.getStatus(), mockResponse.getStatus());
        assertEquals(realResponse.getBody(), mockResponse.getBody());
    }
}
```

**Contract Testing with Mocks**

Use contract testing to ensure mocks stay in sync with real services:

```java
// Consumer-driven contract test
@Test
public void verifyContract() {
    // Consumer expectations
    ConsumerPact pact = ConsumerPactBuilder
        .consumer("UserService")
        .hasPactWith("UserAPI")
        .uponReceiving("a request for user 1")
        .path("/users/1")
        .method("GET")
        .willRespondWith()
        .status(200)
        .body(new PactDslJsonBody()
            .integerType("id")
            .stringType("name")
            .stringType("email"))
        .toPact();

    // Verify provider meets contract
    pactVerifier.verifyProvider()
        .withProvider("UserAPI")
        .withPact(pact)
        .verify();

    // Now you can confidently mock according to contract
    stubFor(get("/users/1")
        .willReturn(okJson("{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"John\",\"email\":\"john@test.com\"}")));
}
```

