**Performance Testing Strategy**

```java
public class PerformanceTestStrategy {

    @Test
    public void performanceTestWithMocks() {
        // Use fast mocks to isolate our code's performance
        stubFor(get("/database")
            .willReturn(ok().withFixedDelay(50)));

        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
            myService.process();
        }
        long duration = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;

        // This measures our code + mock delay
        // Subtract mock delay to get our code's performance
        long ourCodeTime = duration - (1000 * 50);
        assertTrue(ourCodeTime < 500, "Our code too slow");
    }

    @Test
    @EnabledInEnvironment("PERF")
    public void endToEndPerformanceTest() {
        // Use real services to measure actual performance
        // Run fewer iterations, monitor carefully
        PerformanceResult result = performanceTestRunner
            .withThreads(10)
            .withDuration(Duration.ofMinutes(5))
            .withRealServices(true)
            .run();

        assertTrue(result.getP95ResponseTime() < 1000);
    }
}
```

**Mock Verification Strategy**

Regularly verify that mocks still match reality:

```java
@Scheduled(cron = "0 0 * * *") // Daily
public void verifyMocksAgainstReal() {
    // Get all active mocks
    List<StubMapping> mocks = wireMock.listAllStubMappings().getMappings();

    for (StubMapping mock : mocks) {
        try {
            // Call real API
            Response realResponse = callRealAPI(mock.getRequest());

            // Call mock
            Response mockResponse = callMockAPI(mock.getRequest());

            // Compare
            if (!responsesMatch(realResponse, mockResponse)) {
                logger.warn("Mock {} no longer matches real API", mock.getId());
                // Optionally update mock
                updateMockFromReal(mock, realResponse);
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.error("Failed to verify mock", e);
        }
    }
}
```

**Decision Framework**

| Test Type | Mock Strategy | Real Service Strategy |
|-----------|---------------|----------------------|
| **Unit Tests** | 100% mock | 0% real |
| **Component Tests** | 80% mock | 20% real (core only) |
| **Integration Tests** | 30% mock | 70% real |
| **E2E Tests** | 0% mock | 100% real (staging) |
| **Performance Tests** | 50% mock | 50% real (isolated) |
| **Security Tests** | 90% mock | 10% real (scoped) |

**Key Insight:** The art of mocking is knowing when to mock and when to use real services. Mocks give you speed and control; real services give you confidence. The optimal strategy uses both—mocks for unit and component tests, real services for integration and E2E tests, and a verification process to ensure mocks stay accurate.

Mocks are powerful, but they can become a maintenance burden if not managed properly. These best practices ensure your mocks remain valuable assets rather than technical debt.

**1. Keep Mocks Simple**

```java
// BAD: Overly complex mock
stubFor(post("/complex")
    .withRequestBody(matchingJsonPath("$.user.profile.settings.notifications.email"))
    .withRequestBody(matchingJsonPath("$.user.profile.settings.notifications.sms"))
    .withHeader("X-Correlation-ID", matching("[a-f0-9-]+"))
    .withHeader("Authorization", containing("Bearer"))
    .withQueryParam("version", equalTo("v2"))
    .willReturn(aResponse()
        .withTransformers("complex-transformer")
        .withTransformerParameter("dbLookup", true)
        .withFixedDelay(calculateDelay())));

// GOOD: Simple, focused mock
stubFor(post("/users")
    .withRequestBody(matchingJsonPath("$.email"))
    .willReturn(created()));
```

**2. Document Mock Behavior**

```java
public class PaymentServiceMocks {

    /**
     * Configures mocks for happy path payment processing.
     * Payment gateway returns success for amounts < 1000.
     * Fraud service returns low risk for valid cards.
     */
    public static void setupHappyPathMocks() {
        stubFor(post("/payment/process")
            .withRequestBody(matchingJsonPath("$.amount", 
                matching("^([1-9][0-9]{0,2})$")))
            .willReturn(okJson("{\"status\":\"success\"}")));

        stubFor(post("/fraud/check")
            .willReturn(okJson("{\"risk\":\"low\"}")));
    }

    /**
     * Simulates payment gateway timeout.
     * Used for testing retry logic and circuit breakers.
     */
    public static void setupTimeoutScenario() {
        stubFor(post("/payment/process")
            .willReturn(aResponse()
                .withFixedDelay(30000)));
    }
}
```

**3. Version Control Mocks**

```java
// Store mock configurations in version control
public class MockVersioning {

    private static final String MOCK_VERSION = "v2025-04-01";

    public void setupMocks() {
        // Load mocks based on API version
        if (MOCK_VERSION.equals("v2025-04-01")) {
            setupV1Mocks();
        } else if (MOCK_VERSION.equals("v2025-05-01")) {
            setupV2Mocks();
        }
    }

    @Test
    public void testWithMultipleVersions() {
        // Test with different mock versions
        for (String version : Arrays.asList("v1", "v2", "v3")) {
            loadMocksForVersion(version);
            runCompatibilityTests();
        }
    }
}
```

**4. Isolate Mock Configuration**

```java
public class MockConfiguration {

    // Centralized mock setup
    public static void configureBaseMocks(WireMockServer wireMock) {
        wireMock.stubFor(any(urlPathMatching("/health"))
            .willReturn(ok()));

        wireMock.stubFor(any(urlPathMatching("/metrics"))
            .willReturn(okJson("{\"status\":\"healthy\"}")));
    }

    // Domain-specific mock setups
    public static class UserMocks {
        public static void configure() {
            stubFor(get("/users/1")
                .willReturn(okJson("{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"John\"}")));
        }
    }

    public static class PaymentMocks {
        public static void configure() {
            stubFor(post("/payments")
                .willReturn(created()));
        }
    }
}
```

**5. Test Mock Validity**

```java
@Test
public void verifyMocksAreValid() {
    // Test that mocks return expected responses
    List<StubMapping> mocks = wireMock.listAllStubMappings().getMappings();

    for (StubMapping mock : mocks) {
        RequestPattern request = mock.getRequest();

        // Create minimal valid request
        MockHttpRequest testRequest = createTestRequest(request);

        // Call mock
        Response response = wireMock.call(testRequest);

        // Verify response is valid
        assertTrue(response.getStatus() >= 200 && response.getStatus() < 300,
            "Mock " + mock.getId() + " returned error");

        // Verify response structure if applicable
        if (response.getBody() != null) {
            assertNotNull(response.getBodyAsString());
        }
    }
}
```

**6. Clean Up Between Tests**

```java
public class BaseMockTest {

    @BeforeEach
    public void setUp() {
        // Reset mocks before each test
        wireMock.resetAll();

        // Setup common mocks
        setupCommonMocks();
    }

    @AfterEach
    public void tearDown() {
        // Verify expected interactions
        verifyExpectedInteractions();

        // Reset scenarios
        wireMock.resetScenarios();
    }
}
```

**7. Use Realistic Data**

```java
public class RealisticDataGenerator {

    public static String generateUserResponse(int id) {
        return String.format(
            "{\"id\":%d,\"name\":\"%s\",\"email\":\"%s\",\"created\":\"%s\"}",
            id,
            FAKER.name().fullName(),
            FAKER.internet().emailAddress(),
            FAKER.date().past(365, TimeUnit.DAYS).toInstant().toString()
        );
    }

    public static void setupRealisticMocks() {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
            final int userId = i;
            stubFor(get("/users/" + i)
                .willReturn(okJson(generateUserResponse(userId))));
        }

        stubFor(get("/users")
            .willReturn(okJson(
                IntStream.range(1, 11)
                    .mapToObj(i -> generateUserResponse(i))
                    .collect(Collectors.joining(",", "[", "]")))));
    }
}
```

**8. Mock Edge Cases Explicitly**

```java
@Test
public void testEdgeCases() {
    // Empty response
    stubFor(get("/empty")
        .willReturn(ok()));

    // Null values
    stubFor(get("/null-values")
        .willReturn(okJson("{\"id\":null,\"name\":null}")));

    // Empty array
    stubFor(get("/empty-list")
        .willReturn(okJson("[]")));

    // Very large response
    stubFor(get("/large")
        .willReturn(okJson(generateLargeResponse(10_000))));

    // Special characters
    stubFor(get("/special")
        .willReturn(okJson("{\"text\":\"Hello\nWorld\t\u2603\"}")));
}
```

**9. Monitor Mock Usage**

```java
public class MockMonitor {

    private static final Map<String, Integer> usageCount = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static void trackUsage(String mockName) {
        usageCount.merge(mockName, 1, Integer::sum);
    }

    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 * * *")
    public void reportUsage() {
        usageCount.forEach((mock, count) -> {
            if (count == 0) {
                logger.warn("Mock {} was never used", mock);
            } else if (count > 1000) {
                logger.info("Mock {} used {} times", mock, count);
            }
        });

        // Identify unused mocks for cleanup
        usageCount.clear();
    }
}
```

**10. Mock Behavior Evolution**

```java
public class EvolvingMocks {

    // Phase 1: Development (simple mocks)
    public static void devMocks() {
        stubFor(get("/users/1").willReturn(okJson("{\"id\":1}")));
    }

    // Phase 2: Testing (realistic mocks)
    public static void testMocks() {
        stubFor(get("/users/1")
            .willReturn(okJson("{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"John\",\"email\":\"john@test.com\"}")));
    }

    // Phase 3: Performance (simulated delays)
    public static void perfMocks() {
        stubFor(get("/users/1")
            .willReturn(aResponse()
                .withBody("{\"id\":1}")
                .withRandomDelay(new NormalDistribution(50, 10))));
    }

    // Phase 4: Production-like (full behavior)
    public static void prodLikeMocks() {
        stubFor(get("/users/1")
            .inScenario("User Cache")
            .willReturn(aResponse()
                .withHeader("X-Cache", "MISS")
                .withBody("{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"John\"}"))
            .willSetStateTo("Cached"));

        stubFor(get("/users/1")
            .inScenario("User Cache")
            .whenScenarioStateIs("Cached")
            .willReturn(aResponse()
                .withHeader("X-Cache", "HIT")
                .withBody("{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"John\"}")));
    }
}
```

**Key Insight:** Mocks are code and deserve the same care as production code. Keep them simple, document them, version them, and monitor them. The goal is mocks that are reliable enough to trust, yet flexible enough to evolve as your understanding of the system grows.

Mocha is a feature-rich JavaScript test framework, and Chai is an assertion library. Together with Supertest for HTTP assertions, they form a powerful JavaScript API testing stack.

**Overview**

