**Key Insight:** Subscriptions introduce stateful, long-lived connections to your API. Testing them requires thinking about time, concurrency, and connection lifecycle. The key challenges are ensuring that clients receive the right events at the right time, that filters work correctly, and that connections are cleaned up properly when clients disconnect.

Techniques for measuring and optimizing GraphQL API performance.

**The n+1 Problem Detection**

The classic GraphQL performance issue: a query that triggers N+1 database queries.

```javascript
// n-plus-one.test.js
const { graphql } = require('graphql');
const { schema } = require('./schema');
const { createTestContext, trackQueries } = require('./test-utils');

describe('n+1 Detection', () => {
  test('should detect n+1 queries', async () => {
    const query = `
      query {
        posts {
          title
          author {
            name
          }
        }
      }
    `;

    const context = createTestContext();
    const queryTracker = trackQueries(context);

    await graphql(schema, query, null, context);

    const queries = queryTracker.getQueries();
    const authorQueries = queries.filter(q => q.includes('SELECT.*FROM authors'));

    if (authorQueries.length > 1) {
      console.log(`⚠️ n+1 detected: ${authorQueries.length} author queries for ${queries[0].rowCount} posts`);
    }

    expect(authorQueries.length).toBe(1);
  });
});
```

