**Key Insight:** Each query option must be tested in isolation and in combination. $filter with $orderby, $expand with $select, $top with $skip—the combinations multiply quickly. Prioritize testing the combinations that your actual clients use most frequently.

GraphQL's client-driven model creates a combinatorial explosion of test cases.

**The Query Combinatorics Problem**

Consider a simple User type with ten fields. A client could request any subset of those fields—that's 2^10 (1024) possible field combinations. Add a few nested relationships (posts, comments, friends) and the number becomes astronomical. You can't test every possible query—you must test strategically.

**Schema Testing: Your First Line of Defense**

Before testing queries, test the schema itself. The schema is your contract with clients—if it's broken, nothing else matters.

```javascript
const { buildSchema, validateSchema } = require('graphql');

const schemaSDL = `
  type User {
    id: ID!
    name: String!
    email: String!
    posts: [Post!]!
  }

  type Post {
    id: ID!
    title: String!
    content: String!
    author: User!
  }

  type Query {
    user(id: ID!): User
    posts(limit: Int): [Post!]!
  }
`;

describe('GraphQL Schema', () => {
  test('should be valid', () => {
    const schema = buildSchema(schemaSDL);
    const errors = validateSchema(schema);
    expect(errors).toHaveLength(0);
  });

  test('should have User type with required fields', () => {
    const schema = buildSchema(schemaSDL);
    const userType = schema.getType('User');
    const fields = userType.getFields();

    expect(fields.id).toBeDefined();
    expect(fields.name).toBeDefined();
    expect(fields.email).toBeDefined();
    expect(fields.posts).toBeDefined();

    // Check that id is non-null
    expect(fields.id.type.toString()).toContain('!');
  });
});
```

**Query Pattern Testing**

Instead of testing all possible queries, test representative patterns:

```javascript
describe('GraphQL Queries', () => {
  let server;

  beforeEach(async () => {
    server = await startTestServer();
  });

  test('should fetch single field', async () => {
    const query = `
      query {
        user(id: 1) {
          name
        }
      }
    `;

    const response = await request(server)
      .post('/graphql')
      .send({ query });

    expect(response.body.errors).toBeUndefined();
    expect(response.body.data.user.name).toBeDefined();
    expect(response.body.data.user.id).toBeUndefined();
  });

  test('should fetch multiple fields', async () => {
    const query = `
      query {
        user(id: 1) {
          id
          name
          email
        }
      }
    `;

    const response = await request(server)
      .post('/graphql')
      .send({ query });

    expect(response.body.data.user.id).toBe('1');
    expect(response.body.data.user.name).toBeDefined();
    expect(response.body.data.user.email).toBeDefined();
  });

  test('should fetch nested data', async () => {
    const query = `
      query {
        user(id: 1) {
          name
          posts {
            title
            comments {
              text
              author {
                name
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    `;

    const response = await request(server)
      .post('/graphql')
      .send({ query });

    expect(response.body.data.user.posts[0].comments[0].author.name).toBeDefined();
  });
});
```

**Testing Mutations**

Mutations in GraphQL can modify data and return results in the same request. Test that mutations work and that you can query the results.

```javascript
describe('GraphQL Mutations', () => {
  test('should create user and return data', async () => {
    const mutation = `
      mutation CreateUser($input: UserInput!) {
        createUser(input: $input) {
          id
          name
          email
        }
      }
    `;

    const variables = {
      input: {
        name: 'Test User',
        email: 'test@example.com'
      }
    };

    const response = await request(server)
      .post('/graphql')
      .send({ query: mutation, variables });

    expect(response.body.errors).toBeUndefined();
    expect(response.body.data.createUser.id).toBeDefined();
    expect(response.body.data.createUser.name).toBe('Test User');
  });

  test('should validate input', async () => {
    const mutation = `
      mutation CreateUser($input: UserInput!) {
        createUser(input: $input) {
          id
        }
      }
    `;

    const variables = {
      input: {
        name: '',  // Invalid: empty name
        email: 'not-an-email'
      }
    };

    const response = await request(server)
      .post('/graphql')
      .send({ query: mutation, variables });

    expect(response.body.errors).toBeDefined();
    expect(response.body.errors[0].message).toContain('name');
  });
});
```

**Testing for N+1 Problems**

The classic GraphQL performance issue: a query that triggers N+1 database queries.

```javascript
test('should detect n+1 queries', async () => {
  const query = `
    query {
      posts {
        title
        author {
          name
        }
      }
    }
  `;

  const queryTracker = trackQueries();

  await request(server)
    .post('/graphql')
    .send({ query });

  const queries = queryTracker.getQueries();

  // Should be 1 query for posts + 1 query for authors (batched)
  // If n+1 exists, we'd see posts query + N author queries
  expect(queries.length).toBe(2);
});
```

**Testing Query Complexity**

Prevent clients from running overly expensive queries by testing complexity limits.

```javascript
test('should reject overly complex queries', async () => {
  const query = `
    query {
      users {
        posts {
          comments {
            author {
              posts {
                comments {
                  author {
                    name
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  `;

  const response = await request(server)
    .post('/graphql')
    .send({ query });

  // Should be rejected by depth limiting or complexity analysis
  expect(response.body.errors).toBeDefined();
  expect(response.body.errors[0].message).toMatch(/complex|depth/);
});
```

**Key Insight:** GraphQL testing requires shifting from endpoint thinking to query-pattern thinking. You can't test every possible query, so you test representative patterns, edge cases, and performance characteristics. Schema testing becomes critical—the schema is your contract and your first line of defense.
